Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn's Disease)
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Right lower quadrant pain, Chronic diarrhoea, Perianal fistulas/fissures, Transmural skip lesions on colonoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-037
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting, severe right-sided abdominal pain, extreme bloating, and repeated bilious vomiting indicating a potential bowel obstruction.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, partner.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted acute exacerbation of right lower quadrant pain, high-volume bilious vomiting, and severe bloating in a patient with a history of chronic diarrhoea and perianal disease. This presentation is highly suspicious for an acute stricturing small bowel obstruction secondary to Crohn's disease. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to confirm mechanical obstruction, rule out ischemic compromise, and expedite direct hospital admission for surgical evaluation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My lower right side is in agony, my stomach is swollen like a balloon, and I can't stop throwing up green bile."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 2-year history of chronic insidious non-bloody diarrhoea and recurrent crampy abdominal pain typically localized to the right lower quadrant. Notes significant unintentional weight loss and profound fatigue. Reports a history of chronic, painful perianal skin tags and a complex fistula requiring previous drainage. Over the past 24 hours, the patient developed acute bowel obstruction symptoms, including severe, progressive abdominal bloating, absolute constipation, and high-volume bilious vomiting. Reports low-grade fevers during this current flare.
Functional Status: Bedbound, writhing in pain, unable to tolerate any oral intake.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of severe dehydration, electrolyte derangement, and bowel perforation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 115 | Temp: 37.9 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 60 Kg (Cachectic)
General: Patient appears chronically unwell, cachectic, and in acute distress due to colicky pain.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is markedly distended and tympanic. High-pitched, 'tinkling' bowel sounds auscultated. Palpation reveals a firm, severely tender inflammatory mass in the right iliac fossa. Moderate localized guarding present, but no rigid board-like peritonism. Perianal inspection reveals chronic, complex skin tags and a healed fistula scar. The clinical picture explicitly demonstrates right lower quadrant pain, chronic diarrhoea, and perianal fistulas/fissures; transmural skip lesions on colonoscopy are presumed to be the underlying structural cause of the current stricture.
Environmental Observations: Emesis basin containing green, bilious fluid next to the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Non-adherent to prescribed azathioprine for the past 6 months due to reported side effects.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Crohn's Disease - Status: Exacerbated (Acute small bowel obstruction secondary to fibrostenotic stricture)
Differentials:

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The chronic, 2-year history of right lower quadrant pain, profound weight loss, and the presence of complex perianal disease, which strongly dictate an exacerbation of Crohn's rather than a primary appendiceal event.

Infectious Ileitis (e.g., Yersinia) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The clear presentation of a mechanical bowel obstruction (massive distension, bilious vomiting) and the chronic stigmata of inflammatory bowel disease (perianal fistulas).

Large Bowel Volvulus - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a localized, tender inflammatory mass specifically in the right iliac fossa, combined with the classic Crohn's phenotype.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical risk is critically high)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a classic fibrostenotic complication of uncontrolled Crohn's disease, manifesting as an acute mechanical small bowel obstruction localized to the terminal ileum. The objective findings of a tender right iliac fossa mass, marked abdominal distension, high-pitched bowel sounds, and bilious emesis demand immediate escalation of care. Urgent out-of-hours evaluation was mandatory to confirm the obstructive pathology, determine hemodynamic stability, and immediately arrange emergency ambulance transfer for intravenous fluid resuscitation, nasogastric decompression, and surgical consultation. Delaying assessment would have subjected the patient to an unacceptable risk of bowel ischemia or perforation overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed patient strictly nil by mouth (NBM). Contacted emergency services (000) for priority ambulance transport. Administered IM Ondansetron 4mg to attempt to reduce further emesis pending paramedic arrival.
Positive Behavioral Change: Do not consume any food or water, and do not take any oral pain relief or laxatives while waiting for the ambulance. Keeping your stomach and bowel entirely empty mechanically prevents further violent contractions against the blocked section of your intestine, drastically reducing the risk of the bowel wall tearing or bursting.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None currently; requires inpatient surgical management.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance is en route. If the pain suddenly changes from waves of cramps to a constant, agonizing, tearing sensation across the entire stomach, or if the patient becomes unresponsive, the partner must immediately update the 000 dispatcher to upgrade the priority.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a surgical emergency requiring immediate hospital admission.
Clinical Course: The bowel obstruction requires urgent hospital management, which will include a tube through the nose to drain the stomach, intravenous steroids, and potentially emergency surgery to remove the scarred section of the bowel.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to push through the pain or use a heating pad. The bowel is physically blocked by severe scarring and inflammation.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Years of chronic inflammation in the lower right part of your bowel have caused severe, thick scar tissue to form (a stricture). This scar tissue has now completely closed off the tube, acting like a concrete dam. The bilious vomiting occurs because the digestive fluids have nowhere to go but backward. The hospital will use a nasogastric tube to drain this fluid and rest the bowel, followed by powerful intravenous medications to reduce the swelling, or surgery to cut out the blockage.
Actionable Steps: Inform the admitting surgical team immediately about your long-standing history of perianal fistulas and right lower quadrant pain. This specific history is critical for them to recognize this as a severe Crohn's complication rather than a simple appendicitis, directly changing the surgical approach required.

